INT. BARBERSHOP - AFTERNOON

A narrow frontier shop with a tin ceiling, a single leather chair, and a window papered with hand-lettered notices. A pot-bellied stove ticks in the corner. Dust motes drift through slanting light. Outside, a wagon rattles by and fades.

JOSIAH CRANE-OLUWASEUN (50s, barber, white apron, razor in a steady hand) stands behind the chair, lathering a brush. In the chair, face tilted to the ceiling and a towel tucked under his chin, sits JUDGE EUSTACE VANDERMEER (60s, silver mustache, pocket watch, boots propped on the footrest). By the window, DEPUTY NED PRUETT (20s, star too bright on his vest, a rifle he does not know how to hold) leans against the frame, picking at a splinter.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
You're quiet today, Josiah.

JOSIAH
It's a quiet town, Judge.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
It was louder before the railroad stopped.

JOSIAH
It was.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Funny how a place can go still all at once.

JOSIAH
Not all at once. A little each day.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
You're a philosopher.

JOSIAH
I'm a barber.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Same thing, with sharper tools.

Josiah brushes lather across the Judge's jaw. The stove ticks. Ned shifts at the window.

NED
Judge, the stage comes through at four.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
So I've heard.

NED
Is it true the man on it is--

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Deputy. Hush.

NED
Sorry, sir.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
We do not discuss prisoners until they are in the cell.

NED
Yes, sir.

JOSIAH
Ned, bring that chair over. You're making the lamp nervous.

Ned drags a stool closer. It scrapes. The Judge winces.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Josiah, how long have you been shaving me?

JOSIAH
Twenty-one years.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Twenty-one. Have I ever once told you thank you?

JOSIAH
Not that I recall.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Then thank you.

JOSIAH
You're welcome.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Now tell me what you know about the man on the stage.

Josiah stops. The razor hovers at the Judge's throat.

JOSIAH
I know he used to sit in that chair.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
So you do.

JOSIAH
I know he asked me once for the closest shave I could give him. Said a man should face his end clean.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Did you give it to him?

JOSIAH
I did.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Hm.

Josiah resumes. The blade draws a slow line along the Judge's cheek. Ned watches, open-mouthed.

NED
Sir, are you not afraid?

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Of a razor?

NED
Of the man.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Son, in my line of work you learn that the man is never the thing. It is what he's carrying.

The bell above the door jingles. Everyone freezes.

A woman stands in the doorway, backlit, her silhouette sharp against the white glare. BELLE ASHBY-OKONKWO (30s, riding coat, wide hat, a leather satchel on one hip) steps inside. Dust trails off her boots. She removes her hat and shakes loose a mane of black hair.

BELLE
Gentlemen. I'm told this is the place for a shave.

JOSIAH
We're closed to ladies.

BELLE
Then I'll be the first exception.

She sits on the stool Ned vacated. Crosses her legs. Looks at the Judge in the chair, the razor at his throat.

BELLE (CONT'D)
Judge Vandermeer. I wondered when I'd find you.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Miss Ashby.

BELLE
It's been a long road from Dry Creek.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
I imagine it has.

BELLE
My brother was on the stage this morning.

Ned straightens. His hand drifts to the rifle.

NED
Ma'am, I'm sorry, but--

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Deputy. Put the rifle down.

NED
Judge?

JUDGE VANDERMEER
She's not armed. Not yet.

Belle smiles thinly. She sets the satchel on her lap and unfastens it. The leather creaks. Josiah's razor trembles against the Judge's skin.

JOSIAH
Miss, whatever's in that bag--

BELLE
It's a pardon, Mr. Crane. Signed by the governor. For my brother.

Silence. The stove ticks. Josiah slowly lowers the razor.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
(carefully)
May I see it?

BELLE
You may.

She holds out a folded paper. The Judge takes it with two fingers. Opens it. Reads. His face does not change, but his hand does.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
This is genuine.

BELLE
It is.

JUDGE VANDERMEER
Then he's free.

BELLE
He's been free since Tuesday. I wanted you to hear it from me.

Ned lowers the rifle to the floor. The Judge lets the paper fall into his lap and closes his eyes. Josiah stands behind him, razor held still.

The stove ticks. A wagon passes. Outside, in the long afternoon, a stage pulls up and stops.
